To generate a table of contents (TOC) for a LaTeX document, you can use the `toc` package along with some custom commands to create a structured list of sections and subsections. Here’s an example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}

% Define the TOC command
\newcommand{\toc}[2]{
    \setcounter{secnumdepth}{0} % Set depth to 0 for no section numbering
    \setcounter{tocdepth}{#1} % Set depth to #1
    \tableofcontents
}

% Example usage
\begin{document}

\section*{Introduction}
This is the introduction.

\subsection*{Section 1}
This is the first subsection.

\subsubsection*{Subsection 1.1}
This is the first subsubsection.

\subsubsection*{Subsection 1.2}
This is the second subsubsection.

\end{document}
```

### Explanation:
- **`\toc` Command**: This defines a new command `\toc`. It sets the depth of the toc to zero (`\setcounter{secnumdepth}{0}`), which means there will be no section numbers.
- **`\tableofcontents`**: This loads the `toc` package and generates the TOC.
- **Example Usage**: In the main body of the document, we define two sections and their respective subsections using the `\section`, `\subsection`, and `\subsubsection` commands. Each section has its own subsection within the same level as the parent section.

This setup ensures that the TOC includes all relevant sections and subsections without manual intervention. You can adjust the depth levels according to your needs or customize the appearance further if desired.

