It is good practice to use == for comparing objects that have the same type (e.g., Strings). It will work fine if you're comparing two String objects with the same value. If you want to check whether an object has the same value as another object, you can use the equals method. The equals method returns true if the two objects refer to the same object, and false otherwise. Here's an example:

```
String s1 = "Hello";
String s2 = "Hello";

if(s1.equals(s2)) {
    System.out.println("The strings are equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("The strings are not equal");
}
```

In this case, since both s1 and s2 contain the string "Hello", they are considered equal by the equals method. So, the output would be "The strings are equal".

On the other hand, if you were comparing two different types of objects, like Integer and String, then you'd need to use the == operator. For example:

```
int i1 = 5;
Integer i2 = new Integer(5);

if(i1 == i2) {
    System.out.println("The integers are equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("The integers are not equal");
}
```

Here, even though i1 and i2 are both instances of Integer, they don't refer to the same object because they are different types. Therefore, the output would be "The integers are not equal". 

So, while == is generally a good choice when comparing objects of the same type, it's important to note that it doesn't always work correctly when dealing with different types of objects. Using the equals method is recommended whenever possible.

